  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nMoving into the middle of the week, most model agreement does see\nthe upper-level high pressure system stay situated over the \nSouthwestern Untied States. However, with the upper-level trough \nnow well moved off the CONUS, most runs do see the high staying \nelongate back eastwards towards the Panhandles. This elongation \nwill in turn see the Panhandles warm up once again with most of \nthe week and weekend seeing high temperatures near or over the \ntriple-digit mark. With this continuous heat present for such a \nlong period, the Panhandles will need to watch for the need of \nheat related products, especially in areas like the Palo Duro \nCanyon where temperatures can get hotter than expected. One silver\nlining to this pattern, however, is that it does opens up us to a\nmore northwesterly upper-level flow on occasion. This flow is \nbeneficial in that it can make it easier for weak short-waves to \nbrush the Panhandles, or provide enough steering flow to seeing \nstorms developing off the Southern Front Range push into the \nWestern Panhandles. Presently chances of storms are present each \nafternoon and evening. However, these chances are only 20 to 30 \npercent at their best.",
